(*
expect:
one row 113
overflowing rows 8
frame fits true
frame bytes 485
frame entries 4
dropped the tail true
kept the lowest seqs true
one more would not fit true
*)
(* rete#161 — the tail-trim keeps the LONGEST PREFIX that fits, and this pins that
   as a property rather than as an implementation.

   WHY IT EXISTS. `pushFrame`'s trim used to walk: drop the last row, re-encode the
   whole frame, repeat. rete#161 measured that walk at 62% of a check-in; rete#199
   measured what it does to a node whose pushes are being REFUSED, where `acked`
   freezes and every check-in re-collects everything — ~80 full-frame encodes twice
   a second, each abandoning a ladder of power-of-two ByteBuilder buffers, until the
   arena could not find 4104 contiguous bytes and the node halted with 34% of it
   live. The walk is now a bisection.

   A BISECTION IS ONLY LEGAL IF THE PREDICATE IS MONOTONE, and it is — not by
   assumption about the data but by construction of the codec: `Cbor>>encodeList:`
   emits the array head and then encodes each element independently, so a row costs
   the same bytes alone as in place, and adding a row can only lengthen the frame.
   This file is the guard on that: it pins the trim's OUTPUT, so a future change to
   either the codec or the trim that breaks the equivalence shows up as a number
   moving rather than as a frame quietly losing rows.

   THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED, NOT DERIVED, and they were confirmed identical against
   the pre-bisection walk before being pinned — which is what makes this a
   refactor guard and not a restatement of whatever the code now does.

   WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TEST. Which rows the trim drops is rete#150's
   question and is unchanged here: it still takes the TAIL, so it still drops the
   freshest, highest-seq row first. `dropped the tail` and `kept the lowest seqs`
   below pin that rule so a bisection bug that inverted the prefix could not pass.

   Eight obs cells is the ceiling `maxRows` allows from one prefix, and each value
   is sized so the eight together overflow 512 B by enough that the trim must drop
   more than one row — a one-row trim would pass under a walk and a bisection
   alike, and prove nothing about either. *)
class TrimIsMaximal packageUsing: manifest = () (

  (* A value fat enough that eight rows overflow the 512 B cap by SEVERAL rows and
     not by one. A frame that trims a single row is trimmed identically by a walk
     and by a bisection, so it would pass either way and discriminate nothing;
     dropping four is what makes the two algorithms disagree if the bisection's
     bounds are wrong. Fixed content, so the byte counts below are stable, and
     comfortably under the 256 B `maxValueBytes` cap that would reject it outright. *)
  base = ( ^ 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789abcdefghijklmn' )
  filler = ( ^ self base , self base )

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bridge spans frame ent i lo maxSeq minSeq |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: nil space: space id: 'n1' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans.

    (* One row's cost, so the expectations below can be read rather than trusted. *)
    ('one row ' , (platform Cbor encode:
        (platform List with: 1 with: 'obs/c1' with: 2 with: self filler with: 0)) byteSize printString) out.

    i:: 1.
    [ i <= 8 ] whileTrue: [
      space put: ('obs/c' , i printString) value: self filler.
      i:: i + 1 ].

    (* What the collector offers before the trim: maxRows per prefix, and obs is
       the only prefix written here. *)
    ('overflowing rows ' , ((space channel: 'obs') changedSince: 0 max: 8) size printString) out.

    frame:: platform Cbor decode: bridge pushFrame ifInvalid: [ :m | nil ].
    ent:: frame at: #ent ifAbsent: [ nil ].
    ('frame fits ' , ((platform Cbor encode: frame) byteSize <= 512) printString) out.
    ('frame bytes ' , (platform Cbor encode: frame) byteSize printString) out.
    ('frame entries ' , ent size printString) out.

    (* The tail rule (rete#150), pinned so an inverted prefix cannot pass. *)
    minSeq:: (ent at: 1) at: 1.
    maxSeq:: (ent at: ent size) at: 1.
    ('dropped the tail ' , (ent size < 8) printString) out.
    ('kept the lowest seqs ' , (minSeq < maxSeq) printString) out.

    (* MAXIMALITY: the frame is the LONGEST fitting prefix, so one more row must
       not fit. Rebuilt through the same envelope pushFrame uses. *)
    lo:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    i:: 1.
    [ i <= (ent size + 1) ] whileTrue: [
      lo add: (platform List with: i with: 'obs/c' , i printString with: 2 with: self filler with: 0).
      i:: i + 1 ].
    ('one more would not fit ' , ((platform Cbor encode: (platform Record tag: #push
        with: #id -> 'n1'
        with: #bid -> bridge bootId
        with: #ent -> (platform List fromCollection: lo))) byteSize > 512) printString) out.
  )
) : (
)
